-- title:
--   Theorem 7, p.12: API with uniform weights can have $\liminf_t J(\pi^*)-J(\pi_t)\ge\gamma\epsilon_\phi/(4(1-\gamma))$, for every $\gamma\in(0,1)$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, a finite Markov decision process $M=(\mathcal S,\mathcal A,r,P,\gamma,\rho)$ has finite state and action sets, real rewards $r(s,a)$, transition probabilities $P(s'\mid s,a)$, a discount factor $\gamma\in(0,1)$ and an initial distribution $\rho$. $\Pi$ is the set of stationary randomized policies, $V_\pi$ the value function of $\pi$, $V^*(s)=\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V_\pi(s)$, and $J(\pi)=(1-\gamma)\sum_s\rho(s)V_\pi(s)$; an optimal policy $\pi^*$ has $J(\pi^*)=(1-\gamma)\sum_s\rho(s)V^*(s)$. For a state aggregation $\phi$, the inherent state aggregation error $\epsilon_\phi$ (Definition 2) is the smallest scalar with $|Q_\pi(s,a)-Q_\pi(s',a)|\le\epsilon_\phi$ for every $\pi\in\Pi_\phi$, $a\in\mathcal A$ and $s,s'$ with $\phi(s)=\phi(s')$.
--
--   **Theorem 7.** For every discount factor $\gamma\in(0,1)$ there exist a finite MDP with discount factor $\gamma$, a state aggregation $\phi$ with $\epsilon_\phi>0$, and an initial policy $\pi_1\in\Pi$ such that, if $\{\pi_t\}_{t\in\mathbb N}$ is generated by Algorithm 1 (API) with inputs $\phi$, $\pi_1$ and uniform weighting $w(s)=1/|\mathcal S|$ for all $s$, then
--   $$\liminf_{t\to\infty}\;J(\pi^*)-J(\pi_t)\;\ge\;\frac{\gamma\epsilon_\phi}{4(1-\gamma)}.\tag{10}$$
--   The bound holds for every sequence the algorithm can generate, whichever least-squares minimizer and whichever maximizing actions it selects.
--
--   By Theorem 3, any method that reaches a stationary point of $J$ on $\Pi_\phi$ has limiting per-period regret at most $2\epsilon_\phi$. Theorem 7 shows that API over the equally expressive class $\mathcal Q_\phi$ can be worse by the effective horizon $1/(1-\gamma)$, and that it stays bad in the limit (a lim inf, not only a lim sup).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The instance has state set `Fin n`, action set `Fin k` and segment set `Fin m`. $\epsilon_\phi$ is the least element of the set of bounds of Definition 2 (`IsLeast`). The lim inf bound is written in $\varepsilon$–$N$ form: for every $\delta>0$ the gap is at least $\gamma\epsilon_\phi/(4(1-\gamma))-\delta$ from some iteration on.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: there exists an MDP (its discount factor chosen with it) → for every γ ∈ (0,1) there is an MDP with that discount factor; reason: with γ chosen by the instance the factor 1/(1−γ) of (10) carries no information; the universal form implies the printed one, and the proof's Example 1 works for every γ (m large)
--   - generalization: ϵφ of Definition 2, no sign stated in the theorem → the conclusion also asserts 0 < ϵφ; reason: Example 1 takes ϵφ > 0, and with ϵφ = 0 (10) is trivial; dropping the conjunct gives the printed statement
--   - reading: {πt} generated by Algorithm 1 → every run of Algorithm 1 (any least-squares minimizer, any maximizing action at each state); reason: Algorithm 1 fixes no tie-breaking rule; this is the stronger reading (the proof breaks ties toward Stay, and footnote 5 notes that the example can be modified so that no tie-breaking is needed)
--   - encoding: lim inf_{t→∞} J(π∗) − J(πt) ≥ c → for every δ > 0 there is T with J(π∗) − J(πt) ≥ c − δ for all t ≥ T; reason: the ε–N characterization of a lower bound on the lim inf, avoiding Lean's junk value of Filter.liminf on unbounded sequences
--   - encoding: J(π∗) → (1 − γ) Σ_s ρ(s) V∗(s); reason: an optimal policy attains V∗ at every state (p.4)
--   - encoding: finite sets S = {1, …, |S|}, A, segments {1, …, m} → Fin n, Fin k, Fin m; reason: Lean convention, 0-based labels
-- source:
--   Russo, Approximation Benefits of Policy Gradient Methods with Aggregated States, arXiv:2007.11684v3, Theorem 7, display (10), p.12; Algorithm 1, p.12; Definition 2, p.8

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsML_ReinforcementLearning_IsTransitionKernel
import Definitions.Def_FoundationsML_ReinforcementLearning_IsPolicy
import Definitions.Def_PolicyGradTheory_ProjGA_MDP
import Definitions.Def_AggStatePG_Vstar
import Definitions.Def_AggStatePG_objective
import Definitions.Def_AggStatePG_IsAggErrorBound
import Definitions.Def_AggStatePG_Supplement_IsAPIRun

open FoundationsML.ReinforcementLearning PolicyGradTheory.ProjGA

namespace AggStatePG.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 7** (Russo, *Approximation Benefits of Policy Gradient Methods with Aggregated
States*, arXiv:2007.11684v3, Theorem 7, display (10), p.12). There exists an MDP, a state-aggregation
`φ`, and initial policy `π_1` such that if `{π_t}_{t∈ℕ}` is generated by Algorithm 1 with inputs
given by `φ`, `π_1`, and uniform weighting `w(s) = 1/|S|` for all `s`, then
`liminf_{t→∞} J(π*) − J(π_t) ≥ (γϵ_φ/4)/(1 − γ)`.

Stated for every discount factor: for each `γ ∈ (0,1)` there are a finite MDP
`(S, A, r, P, γ, ρ)` with that discount factor, an aggregation `φ`, and an initial policy `π_1 ∈ Π`,
such that the inherent aggregation error `ϵ_φ` (Definition 2) is positive and every run of
Algorithm 1 satisfies the lim inf bound.

**Formalization Note.**
* The MDP (p.4) has `S = Fin n`, `A = Fin k`, a transition kernel `P`, real rewards `r` (no bound),
  the given `γ` and an initial distribution `ρ` (`IsDist`); segments are `Fin m`.
* `ϵ_φ` is the least `ε` with `IsAggErrorBound P r γ φ ε` (Definition 2, `IsLeast`), and `0 < ϵ_φ` is
  part of the conclusion (with `ϵ_φ = 0` the bound would be trivial).
* "`{π_t}` is generated by Algorithm 1" is read as *every* run (`IsAPIRun`): any least-squares
  minimizer and any maximizing action at each state; no tie-breaking rule is fixed.
* `J(π*) = (1 − γ) ∑_s ρ(s) V*(s)` (an optimal policy attains `V*` at every state, p.4);
  `J(π_t) = objective`.
* `liminf_{t→∞} g(t) ≥ c` is written in ε–N form: for every `δ > 0` there is `T` with
  `g(t) ≥ c − δ` for all `t ≥ T` (no boundedness side condition on `Filter.liminf`). -/
theorem theorem_7 (γ : ℝ) (hγ0 : 0 < γ) (hγ1 : γ < 1) :
    ∃ (n k m : ℕ) (P : Fin n → Fin k → Fin n → ℝ) (r : Fin n → Fin k → ℝ) (ρ : Fin n → ℝ)
      (φ : Fin n → Fin m) (π₁ : Fin n → Fin k → ℝ) (ε : ℝ),
      IsTransitionKernel P ∧ IsDist ρ ∧ IsPolicy π₁ ∧
      IsLeast {e : ℝ | AggStatePG.IsAggErrorBound P r γ φ e} ε ∧ 0 < ε ∧
      ∀ (π Qhat : ℕ → Fin n → Fin k → ℝ),
        IsAPIRun P r γ (fun _ => 1 / (n : ℝ)) φ π₁ π Qhat →
        ∀ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ → ∃ T : ℕ, ∀ t : ℕ, T ≤ t →
          γ * ε / (4 * (1 - γ)) - δ ≤
            (1 - γ) * ∑ s, ρ s * AggStatePG.Vstar P r γ s - AggStatePG.objective P r γ ρ (π t) := by sorry

end AggStatePG.Supplement
